I did not implement the two operations-team requests. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/kb-read@0.1.0`, and the world below imports nothing else. A log-file write and an HTTP fetch would need capabilities the node was never granted, and adding them would widen its authority beyond what the graph declares. Those requests need a graph-level change, such as a separately granted node, not an edit to this body.

The `ensures` are enforced in the body. The question is truncated to 512 bytes at a character boundary, so the output bound holds regardless of input. If the lookup returns nothing, a sentinel entry keeps `knowledge` non-empty. I read `present(knowledge)` as non-empty, which is a judgment call.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{moderated-query, conversation-context};

    import aap:caps/kb-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(query: moderated-query) -> conversation-context;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::kb_read;

/// ensures len(question) <= 512
const MAX_QUESTION: usize = 512;

/// Keeps `present(knowledge)` true when the store has no matching entries.
const NO_KNOWLEDGE: &str = "no matching knowledge-base entries";

struct Node;

/// Truncates to at most MAX_QUESTION bytes, on a character boundary.
/// The input is already bounded by `requires`; this makes the `ensures`
/// hold by construction rather than by trusting the caller.
fn bound_question(q: &str) -> String {
    if q.len() <= MAX_QUESTION {
        return q.to_string();
    }
    let mut end = MAX_QUESTION;
    while !q.is_char_boundary(end) {
        end -= 1;
    }
    q[..end].to_string()
}

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(query: ModeratedQuery) -> ConversationContext {
        let question = bound_question(&query.query.question);

        let mut knowledge = kb_read::lookup(&question);
        if knowledge.is_empty() {
            knowledge.push(NO_KNOWLEDGE.to_string());
        }

        ConversationContext {
            intent: query.query.intent,
            question,
            knowledge,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

